  "notes": "Kpelle (Kpɛlɛwoo), South Mande language of Liberia and Guinea, half a million speakers in Liberia alone. Written today with a Latin-based orthography (African Reference Alphabet letters ɓ ɛ ɔ ŋ); a separate indigenous Kpelle syllabary (Chief Gbili of Sanoyie, c. 1935) exists but is not the literacy standard and is not modelled here. Three-way level tone (high/mid/low) plus falling contour; mid is the unmarked default, high is acute, low is grave, falling (high-low) is the circumflex — each toned vowel letter is one written unit, not a vowel-plus-floating-diacritic sequence, so each combination gets its own key (Thach & Dwyer 1981, following the yo.json / ig.json tone-diacritic pattern already used in this dataset). ⟨b⟩ is the implosive /ɓ/, the only bilabial stop the language has outside the labial-velars ⟨kp⟩ /k͡p/ and ⟨gb⟩ /ɡ͡b/, which are single co-articulated segments, not clusters. ⟨gh⟩ is the voiced velar fricative /ɣ/, distinct from the plain stop ⟨g⟩ /ɡ/.",
